{{History of Balisca}}

The '''Confederation of the Northen Provinces''' ({{lang-bl|Confederación Provincias Norte}}), commonly known as the '''Northern Confederation''' ({{Lang-bl|Confederación Norte}}), was the predecessor state to the modern-day [[Baliscan Confederation]]. Although there has been research on the idea of a pan-Baliscan identity that overlapped the early political boundaries, political unification of the archipelago was not the aim of most, nor was it necessarily inevitable. In fact, there was little coordination between the different factions prior to confederation, despite their mutual interest in resisting the ever-present threat of a Andalusian attempt at colonial reconquest. 

[[W:Spain under Joseph Bonaparte|Spain under Joseph Bonaparte]] attempted to forcibly re-impose colonial rule in the Baliscan archipelago, marking the end of the [[Espanya Boba|"Espanya Boba"]] period of Baliscan history stretching back to the early 18th century. The Southern Confederation was established following the issuing of the [[Proclamation of Alcazaba]] between the [[republics of Balisca|republics]] of [[Commonwealth of Cerdanya|Cerdanya]] and [[Hesperia]] against Andalusian reconquest, precipitating the ''[[Baliscan War of Independence|Resistencía]]''.

The Confederation's motto, ''[[W:Pari passu|pari passu]]'', a Mesogean phrase that literally means "with an equal step" or "on equal footing", and sometimes translated as "ranking equally", "hand-in-hand", "with equal force", or "moving together", and by extension, "fairly", "without partiality" reflected the idealist structure of the [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal government]] in relation to the three founding republics. 

